          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong monoidal actegory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudoaction"} pseudoaction
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi MonCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{MonCat}
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functors"} strong monoidal functors
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+natural+transformations"} monoidal natural transformations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actegory"} actegory
            whose
            %em actee
            is monoidal and such that the action is compatible with this structure.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Since pseudoactions are actions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonoids"} pseudomonoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi MonCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{MonCat}
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
          %p
            The following comes from Definition 5.1.1 in (
            %a{:href => "#CG22"}> Capucci and Gavranović 2022
            ):
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{M}, j, \otimes)
              be a braided monoidal category. A left
              %em
                monoidal
                = succeed "-actegory" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℳ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              is a monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, i,\boxtimes)
              together with a strong monoidal functor, called
              = succeed "," do
                %strong action
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot:\mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi j
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mi x
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_x : j \odot x \to x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi m
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_{m,n,x} : (m \otimes n) \odot x \to m \odot (n \odot x)
              satisfying the coherence laws of unitor and multiplicator of an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actegory"} actegory
          %p
            All the coherence laws of a monoidal actegory are spelled out in Appendix A of (
            %a{:href => "#CG22"}> Capucci and Gavranović 2022
            ).
          %p
            The fact
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊙
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
            is strong monoidal means it comes equipped with a structural morphism called
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong mixed interchanger
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\iota_{m,x,n,y} : (m \otimes n) \odot (x \boxtimes y) \to (m \odot x) \boxtimes (n \odot y).
          %p
            It would also come with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi υ
                    %mo :
                    %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi j
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \upsilon : i \to j \odot i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            makes it so that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi υ
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \upsilon = \eta_i
          %h3#braiding Braiding
          %p
            Consider pseudoactions in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi BrMonCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{BrMonCat}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi SymMonCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{SymMonCat}
          %p
            The following appear as 5.4.1 and 5.4.3 in (
            %a{:href => "#CG22"}> Capucci and Gavranović 2022
            ):
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{M}, j, \otimes, \sigma)
              be a symmetric monoidal category. A left
              %strong
                braided monoidal
                = succeed "-actegory" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℳ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              is a braided monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mo ,
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, i, \boxtimes, \beta)
              equipped with a braided monoidal functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot: \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}
              and two monoidal natural transformations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              as above. Thus it satisfies the axioms of monoidal actegory and an additional compatibility axiom with the braided structure of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              and
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %svg{:height => "93.906pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 368.265 93.906", :width => "368.265pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
